    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d212">

                <pb facs="0324" n="226" xml:id="pg226"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">710.11/434</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Salvadoran Minister</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Sol</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-02-26"><hi rendition="#italic">February 26,
                        1920</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of the note Number 752 dated December 14, 1919 from Señor Don Juan Fran<hi
                        rendition="#superscript">co</hi> Paredes, Minister of Foreign Affairs of
                    Salvador in which the Minister of Foreign Affairs requests this Government to
                    set forth its interpretation of the Monroe Doctrine because of the bearing which
                    such interpretation might have on the attitude of the Government of Salvador
                    toward the Covenant of the League of Nations.</p>
                <p>In reply I have the honor to inform you that the views of this government with
                    reference to the Monroe Doctrine were set forth in the address of the President
                    of the United States to the Second Pan American Scientific Congress, copy of the
                    pertinent portions of which I beg to attach herewith.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Frank L. Polk</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d212-a1.xml" xpointer="d212-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
